5. ABI and host-visible behavior
--------------------------------

Implemented:
  - I/O opcode 0x19 as nvme_cmd_copy.
  - NVME_CTRL_ONCS_COPY as bit 8 in Identify Controller ONCS.
  - Command Effects Log entry for Copy with CSUPP | LBCC on NVM/conv builds.
    Numeric observed value is 0x00000003 in this local header:
      NVME_CMD_EFFECTS_CSUPP = 1 << 0
      NVME_CMD_EFFECTS_LBCC  = 1 << 1
  - Identify Namespace Copy limits at raw NVMe Identify Namespace offsets:
      offset 74: MSSRL = 1024
      offset 76: MCL   = 1024
      offset 80: MSRC  = 127
  - Identify Controller Copy Descriptor Format support bit for Format 0h using
    the existing local reserved byte at raw offset 534.
  - ZNS and KV do not advertise Copy.

Important admin.c runtime fix added during smoke testing:
  - Get Log Page previously ignored LPO (log page offset).
  - This caused explicit offset reads of Command Effects Log to return the
    first admin-effects page instead of the requested I/O-effects page.
  - Added __copy_log_page() to honor le64_to_cpu(cmd->lpo), cap copy to one
    page, zero-fill beyond available source bytes, and avoid stack-allocating
    struct nvme_effects_log.
  - Command Effects Log is now built dynamically and uses
    nvmev_copy_supported_ns(&nvmev_vdev->ns[nsid]) instead of relying only on
    static preprocessor initializers.

Residual admin log limitation:
  - Explicit offset read works:
      nvme get-log /dev/nvme2 --log-id=5 --log-len=512 --lpo=1024 --raw-binary
      copy opcode-relative dword 0x64 = 00000003
  - Earlier full 4096-byte raw read still showed 0 at absolute offset 0x464.
    This appears related to existing admin PRP/log transfer behavior or
    nvme-cli/libnvme chunking. It was not fully fixed in this pass.
  - The next agent should retest full-log behavior if full Command Effects
    dump fidelity matters. The conformance smoke used explicit LPO chunking.


6. Command scope
----------------

Accepted:
  - same-namespace Simple Copy,
  - Copy Descriptor Format 0h,
  - 1 to 128 source ranges,
  - at most 1024 LBAs per source range,
  - at most 1024 LBAs total per command,
  - FUA and Limited Retry bits are accepted; only FUA affects conv target
    completion timing.

Rejected with status:
  - unsupported target namespace: Invalid Opcode,
  - invalid namespace: Invalid Namespace,
  - nonzero reserved/protection/metadata/directive fields: Invalid Field,
  - unsupported Copy Descriptor Format: Invalid Field,
  - oversized range count or copy length: Command Size Limit Exceeded,
  - source/destination out of namespace bounds: LBA Out of Range,
  - any source/destination overlap: Overlapping Range,
  - descriptor PRP mapping failure: Data Transfer Error.


7. Descriptor and worker safety invariants
------------------------------------------

Key invariant:
  Descriptor PRPs are decoded once at dispatch time. The decoded range snapshot
  is then owned by the work item until the worker performs or discards the
  operation.

Purpose:
  Prevent host-memory time-of-check/time-of-use mismatch where FTL validates
  one descriptor set but worker copies another descriptor set.

Ownership paths:
  - Success:
      FTL stores decoded copy_ranges in nvmev_result.
      __enqueue_io_req() transfers ownership to nvmev_io_work.
      Worker frees after payload copy.
  - Validation failure:
      no snapshot is attached; status-gated worker performs no payload movement.
  - Work queue allocation failure:
      __nvmev_proc_io() frees the snapshot before returning false.
  - Defensive path:
      worker frees any unexpected snapshot when status is non-success.

Worker data movement changes:
  - w->status != NVME_SC_SUCCESS now prevents all host/device data movement.
  - Copy uses __do_perform_copy(), not PRP read/write paths.
  - Invalid namespace is checked before indexing nvmev_vdev->ns[nsid].
  - io_size accounting is opcode-aware:
      failures: 0
      Copy: accepted total copied bytes
      flush and unknown non-data commands: 0
      read/write/zone append: existing size accounting


8. NVM and conventional FTL model
---------------------------------

NVM target:
  - schedules each source range as read latency,
  - schedules destination range as contiguous write latency after all sources,
  - performs exact host-visible backing-memory copy in worker.

Conventional FTL target:
  - validates full command before FTL mutation,
  - allocates write buffer before FTL mutation,
  - charges source NAND read timing for mapped/valid source pages,
  - charges internal write-buffer timing without PCIe transfer,
  - invalidates/remaps/programs destination pages,
  - schedules write-buffer release for full and partial pending program groups,
  - performs exact host-visible backing-memory copy in worker.

Model boundary:
  Backing memory copy is exact at LBA byte granularity. FTL timing and metadata
  remain page-level, matching the existing page-mapped conv model. Partial-page
  Copy charges touched page cost. Describe this as model granularity, not
  hardware truth.


9. ZNS/KV cleanup
-----------------

ZNS and KV do not implement Copy.

Their default unknown-opcode paths now return NVME_SC_INVALID_OPCODE instead
of falling through as success. This avoids false-positive results outside the
Simple Copy scope.


